WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Manually building a React Native Android app is an exercise in patience.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
You compile, you wait, you drag files around, you upload.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Today, we are replacing that manual assembly line with a fully automated pipeline.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
By the end of this setup, every pull request will be automatically checked for errors

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
and every merge to the main branch will deploy your app directly to the Google Play Store.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Before we write a single line of configuration, you need three things ready to go.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.000
A GitHub repository, your Android release keystore file, and a Google Play service account JSON key.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Pay close attention to how you format your base 64 encoding and how you spell your repository secrets.

9
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.000
These two steps account for nearly all deployment failures.

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Taking a few minutes to strictly configure this pipeline right now will save you hundreds of hours

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
of staring at loading bars in the future. Let's get to work.

12
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.000
To sign your Android app, GitHub actions needs access to your local keystore file.

13
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
But uploading raw cryptographic files to a repository is a massive security risk,

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
and the runner can't securely read them anyway.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Instead, we need to convert that physical file into a secure text string.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Open your terminal and navigate to the folder containing your keystore.

17
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Run the command base64-i release.keystore.

18
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Using this exact command ensures no invisible spaces or new line characters sneak into the output,

19
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.000
which would instantly corrupt the key.

20
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Copy that block of text, then head over to your GitHub repository.

21
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Navigate to settings, click on Secrets and Variables, and select Actions.

22
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
We need to create five specific repository secrets to match our workflow.

23
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Keystore base64, Keystore password, Key alias, Key password, and Google Play service account.

24
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Paste the terminal output you just generated into the base64 secret.

25
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Then, take the raw text from your Google Play JSON key and paste it directly into the service account secret.

26
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Fill in the remaining passwords and alias from your original local setup.

27
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Double check your spelling and capitalization on these exact secret names.

28
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
A single casing error here means the workflow won't find the key,

29
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.000
and your deployment will fail silently later on.

30
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
With these secrets saved, your remote repository is armed with the cryptographic keys it needs

31
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
to build and publish your app entirely on its own,

32
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.000
keeping your sensitive data completely out of the code base.

33
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Now we can build the actual automation files.

34
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Step 1 is setting up a pull request checker workflow.

35
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.000
In your project folder, create a new file under the .github slash workflows directory,

36
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
called PR-checks.yaml.

37
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:42.000
We will set the trigger condition so this workflow runs anytime someone opens or updates a pull request targeting the main branch.

38
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.000
The core logic here is straightforward.

39
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
The runner will install dependencies, run your linter, execute your test suite,

40
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
and verify that the Android app actually compiles.

41
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Think of this as an automated gatekeeper.

42
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
If a developer pushes code with a syntax error or a failing test,

43
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
this workflow physically blocks the broken code from merging into the main branch.

44
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:11.000
Catching these errors at the pull request stage ensures you never accidentally deploy a broken bill to production.

45
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.000
You are removing human oversight from the equation entirely.

46
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
With the gatekeeper in place, we move to the main event, the build and deploy workflow.

47
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Create a second yaml file, configured to trigger exclusively when code is successfully merged into the main branch.

48
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.000
This workflow will compile a production ready Android app bundle.

49
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
To sign it, the script pulls the repository secrets we saved earlier straight into the runner's environment variables.

50
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Notice how the variables defined in your yaml script map exactly one to one with the secret names you created in the GitHub settings.

51
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Once the bundle is signed, we need to ship it.

52
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.000
For that, we use a trusted open source action called Upload Google Play.

53
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.000
This specific action takes your finished app bundle,

54
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.000
authenticates itself using your service account JSON secret,

55
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.000
and pushes the code directly to Google servers.

56
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.000
You can even specify your track assignment directly in the yaml file.

57
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:11.000
The system automatically assigns the upload to your internal testing, beta, or production tracks in the play console.

58
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:18.000
This single file replaces the tedious developer chore of running local builds, exporting files, logging into web consoles,

59
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
and manually dragging and dropping updates.

60
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.000
There is one major bottleneck to address.

61
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
A fresh react native Android build is incredibly heavy, often taking 20 minutes or more to compile from scratch.

62
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.000
To fix this, we need to cache our dependencies.

63
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.000
By telling GitHub options to store the GradleTache and node modules between runs,

64
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.000
the runner doesn't have to download the required files every time you merge code.

65
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Looking at a standard build time, a properly configured cache can cut an un-cache 25-minute run down to just six minutes.

66
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Implementing a caching strategy is what actually makes CICD pipelines viable.

67
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
If the automated build takes too long, developers get impatient and find ways to bypass the system.

68
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Keep it fast and they'll trust the pipeline.

69
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:10.000
There's nothing quite like clicking merge on a pull request and watching your actions tab light up with a flawless run of green check marks.

70
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:17.000
Within just a few minutes, your new app bundle is sitting in the Google Play console, automatically versioned and ready for testing.

71
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Now, while standard GitHub actions is incredibly capable, some react native teams prefer to offload this work entirely to expose dedicated cloud build service.

72
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.000
We now have our GitHub actions wired up.

73
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Next, click the video on screen to learn to set up EAS build for react native.

